--- appended 2026-09-14: the kind-5 code targets, and where their address actually lives ---
question: generalization 4 lets a kind-5 record point below 'text' (into the segment's own code). What is that
population, and does the existing signed-path arithmetic already express the resulting pointer value?
measurement (--links plus a direct walk, whole corpus, 1255 records with target < text out of 41380 kind-5):
  segment split                      seg0 1051, seg1 204, seg2 0
  distinct target values             626        distinct (text - target)  615
  target % 8 -> {4: 1255}            so text % 8 == 0 for every carrying segment
  patch  % 8 -> {4: 1002, 0: 253}    CORRECTION: earlier notes said all 1255 were patch % 8 == 4.
                                     patch is only guaranteed 4-aligned (claim() checks patch % 4);
                                     253 of 1255 slots sit at % 8 == 0, which is legal for a 4-byte slot.
  patch >= text                      1255 / 1255, so every slot is in signed data as required
  word at seg_base + target, top byte  0x91 x862, 0xf8 x283, 0xd2 x72, 0xd1 x16, 0x90 x16, 0xf0 x4, 0xca x2
                                     = ADD-imm / LDR-STR unsigned-offset / MOVZ / SUB-imm / ADRP / LDR-unscaled,
                                     i.e. real ARM64 instructions at every 4-aligned target; no misalignment.
  members carrying them              task-control 976, default-menu-pc 140, progress-pc 70,
                                     default-menu 64, target-death 4, sage-finalboss 1

finding: the naive widening ('ptr_link_v3 writes target + seg_base, so the result is already a signed code
address') does NOT hold in the signed path. The two regions are disjoint in the common u32 window:
  - the runtime's u32 base is the arena: offset(p) == p - manifest.arena (ottsel_signed_gstate.cpp:41-46), and
    manifest.arena_size == EE_MAIN_MEM_SIZE == 128 MiB.
  - signed text is placed OUTSIDE/after the arena: code = offset(segment.code) and the loader requires
    'code >= manifest.arena_size && segment.text_size <= UINT32_MAX - code' (:96).
  - segment data is placed INSIDE the arena: data_offset >= reservation_begin and
    data_offset + data_size <= reservation_begin + data_span_size, with
    heap_start < reservation_begin < heap_end <= ARENA_SIZE (emit-signed-module-arena.py:135). The shipped
    KERNEL reservation begins 0x3eb4000 (~65.7 MiB), well below 128 MiB.
  So every data offset is < arena_size <= every code offset. goal_offset = data_arena_offset + target -
  text_size describes a data target only; for a code target it lands BELOW the data region, not on the signed
  code. The correct value is offset(segment.code) + target, i.e. the link-time expression code_label + target,
  which the numeric goal_offset field cannot carry. Ordinary ptr_link_v3 (target + seg_base) works only
  because the ordinary linker keeps one module's code and data contiguous from the module base; the signed path
  splits them, so the formula coincides for data targets and diverges for code targets.
four enforced sites, all of which must branch on target < text_size:
  scripts/emit-signed-module-arena.py:117-121 (require text <= target < size; goal_offset == offset + target - text)
    and :156-158 (aggregate assigns goal_offset = cursor + target - text_size)
  scripts/emit-signed-gstate-manifest.py:78-83 (fourth site, called from build-ios.sh:336/344; emits the literal)
  ottsel_signed_gstate.cpp:120-128 (validate) and :264-266 (the actual write of goal_offset into the slot)
  a11_module_emitter.h:172-177 (the guard itself)
consequence: generalization 4 is not a bounds relaxation. Data targets keep the existing formula and equality;
code targets need a distinct goal value resolved from the segment code label at runtime, so the generator asserts,
the emitted literal and the runtime equality all become conditional, and the goal_offset < ARENA_SIZE bound
becomes a signed-text bound instead.
exit 0. No source change, nothing compiled, no build or Simulator.
