## Addendum 2026-09-14 (6) - fail-closed fix shape and the dead S1.3 fallback (read-only)

Source read of `out/jak-project/game/kernel/jak1/kscheme.cpp` (the only jak1 definition site of `find_symbol_from_c`,
:1011; `grep -rln find_symbol_from_c out/jak-project/game` lists only jak2/jak3/jakx plus this file) and
`out/jak-project/game/kernel/common/ottsel_signed_gstate.cpp`.

The non-creating miss path the fix needs is real:
  - `find_symbol_in_area` (:980) records the first blank slot in `symbol_slot` and returns
    `find_symbol_in_fixed_area(hash, name)` (:997 -> :962-971), which returns `Ptr<Symbol>(0)` when the name is absent;
    only a region overflow returns the `Ptr<Symbol>(1)` sentinel (:1002).
  - so :1028-1061 yields a real symbol (present), 0 (absent) or the fixed-area result, and nothing else.

Dead code, verified structurally:
  - `awk 'NR>=1011 && NR<=1065'` prints the whole if/else with no preprocessor directive inside it and all six exits are
    `return` (:1032, :1040, :1042, :1049, :1057, :1059), so the `#ifdef OTTSEL_IOS` full-table fallback (:1062-1085)
    is unreachable; the pristine `ref/open-goal-jak-project/repo/game/kernel/jak1/kscheme.cpp:719` shows the same
    `} }` closing the if/else, i.e. `r0-call-trace-probe-goalc.patch:421` inserted the block after the last statement.
  - no `#define return` exists (`grep -rn 'define return' out/jak-project/game out/jak-project/common src` is empty).
  - consequence: the S1.3 order-independence property is structural (the table never deletes, so insertion and lookup
    walk the same probe chain) and was not supplied by that block; the loader fix must not rely on it.

Interning side effects the fix replaces (ottsel_signed_gstate.cpp :274-290):
  - :285 `auto sym = jak1::intern_from_c(slot.name);`
  - :286-287 the five `diagnostic_names` are captured only because the slot happened to name them.
  - :288 `value = slot.kind == SlotKind::SymbolAddress ? sym.offset : sym.offset - s7.offset;`
  - `intern_from_c` :1090-1111 sets the Type tag (`symbol.cast<u32>().c()[-1]`), `str`, `hash` and `NumSymbols++`;
    `value` is untouched, so a fresh symbol reads 0 from the zero-filled GOAL heap.
  - `intern_from_c` also never requires `symbol_slot != 0`: after both regions overflow (:1037, :1055) with `symbol_slot`
    still 0 from :1012, :1095 writes the Type tag at 4 bytes below GOAL address 0, outside the EE window. Same missing
    `require` class as the value-0 intern; not reachable at the current 279-slot registry.

Type slots stay on the sibling: `intern_type_from_c` :1141-1185 reads `symbol->value` and on 0 calls `alloc_and_init_type`
(:1168), which sets it; a later intern of the same name returns the existing Type unless the requested method count is
larger (:1176-1182). A find-only require there would be wrong rather than safer.

Fix shape and the open product item: `evidence/A1/a11-loader-fail-closed-2026-09-14.md`.

Addendum 6 continued - `return false` is not fail-closed here: `ottsel::signed_gstate::link`'s only two early exits are
`:255` (no name) and `:258` (name absent from the registry), and `link_and_exec` (`jak1/klink.cpp:936-940`) falls
through to the legacy `link_control` path (:951-957, `return lc.m_entry` :983) that links and runs the object from the
raw data buffer - the executable-page route. So `return false` is correct only for not-a-signed-member (level art, `.TXT`,
still-unsigned objects); a registry member with a missing dependency must abort with the offending name, like the
loader's existing `require(...)` checks, which also means `fail()`/`require()` need a formatted variant to carry it.

