                             BucketCategory::OTHER, BucketId::MERC_EYES_AFTER_PRIS);  // 54
  EyeRenderer.h:23-25  handle_eye_dma2 + lookup_eye_texture(u8 eye_id) + ..._hash(hash, lr)
  EyeRenderer.h:53-62  GpuEyeTex { gpu_tex, tbp, FramebufferTexturePair fb(128,128,...) }
                       m_gpu_eye_textures[NUM_EYE_PAIRS*2]  == 40 slots
  TextureUploadHandler.cpp:58-61  sniffs dma_tag.qwc == 128/16 as eye data inside the
                       pris-tex0/pris-tex1 texture-upload buckets, then handle_eye_dma2.

Conclusion: the native fix is a bucket-54 (plus 48/51) GS consumer that executes render-eyes'
sprite pass into a 128x128 target and publishes it atomically with MERC geometry/camera.
No level serialization change and no texture-id swap are required; the eye adgifs ride the DMA.
Addendum 7 note - exact parity binding rule:
  EyeRenderer.cpp:591-604  lookup_eye_texture(u8 eye_id): eye_id = (eye_id % 40);
      if (eye_id >= NUM_EYE_PAIRS*2) return {};  return m_gpu_eye_textures[eye_id].gpu_tex;
  => the composed slot IS the level MercDraw.eye_id (Tfrag3Data.h:550-560, 0xff = not an eye),
     filled by EyeRenderer.cpp:16/:257 as pair_idx*2 + lr (pair_idx = y0/SINGLE_EYE_SIZE=32,
     SINGLE_EYE_SIZE=32 s), i.e. GOAL render-eyes' arg2 (0..10) is exactly that pair and the
     scissor scay0 = arg2*32 places the slot at y = eye_id>>1. Native bind rule: index the
     composed target by eye_id, replacing ottsel_mobile_scene_texture_slot(..., tree_tex_id).

Addendum 7 continuation - native publish mechanism constraint (host-only, read-only):
  src/shell/metal_shell.mm:1425  id<CAMetalDrawable> drawable = [self.layer nextDrawable];
  src/shell/metal_shell.mm:1445-1451  directOffscreenTarget: BGRA8Unorm, RenderTarget usage, Private
  src/shell/metal_shell.mm:1457-1466  one MTLRenderPassDescriptor; colorAttachments[0].texture =
      directFixtureOffscreen ? self.directOffscreenTarget : drawable.texture; loadAction Clear,
      storeAction Store; depthAttachment = self.tieDepth (DontCare).
  src/shell/metal_shell.mm:1471  id<MTLRenderCommandEncoder> enc = [cb renderCommandEncoderWithDescriptor:pass];
  src/shell/metal_shell.mm:1476  RenderEncoderEndGuard encoder_guard(enc);   // RAII, src/shell/metal_encoder_guard.h
  src/shell/metal_shell.mm:2546  the frame's only other encoder is a diagnostic MTLBlitCommandEncoder (readback)
  src/shell/metal_shell.mm:2589  [cb presentDrawable:drawable];   :2596  [cb commit];
  grep -rn over src/ for renderCommandEncoder|nextDrawable|presentDrawable returns those sites only.
  => exactly ONE render pass per presented frame, attached to the drawable (or the diagnostic offscreen
     target). Metal cannot sample a texture that is the current pass attachment, so "publish atomically
     with MERC geometry/camera" cannot mean same-pass: the composer must be its own earlier
     MTLRenderCommandEncoder in the same command buffer, before the main pass opens at :1471.

Ordering precedent inside the GS chain (goal_src/jak1/engine/dma/dma-h.gc:219-227):
  48 pris-tex0 / 49 merc-pris0 / 51 pris-tex1 / 52 merc-pris1 / 54 eyes / 55 merc-pris-common.
  Level eyes compose into 48/51, i.e. BEFORE the 49/52 MERC draws that sample them, so the level-eye
  path can compose at the head of the same command buffer with no frame lag; the common-eye path (54)
  is after 49/52 and would carry one frame. Buckets 48/51 are already the native texture-upload window
  (kOttselJak1TextureUploadBuckets, mobile_generic_policy.h:41-51) and the desktop sniffs the eye payload
  there by qwc == 128/16 (TextureUploadHandler.cpp:58-61), so the composer entry point is an already-
  walked bucket. Side note: ottsel_mobile_is_jak1_texture_upload_bucket (mobile_generic_policy.h:45-52)
  has no caller - dead predicate, the array is currently documentation only.

Publish point on the native side: the MERC bind is src/shell/metal_gfx.mm:2150
  ottsel_mobile_scene_texture_slot(texture_base, source_draw.tree_tex_id)
and mobile_scene_policy.h:69-73 makes that a pure offset (level_texture_base + local_texture_id, negative
ids untouched), so the composer needs a bind override keyed by MercDraw.eye_id (Tfrag3Data.h:550-560)
rather than a new serialized level field. First-frame texture growth exists as precedent in the same file
(:1442-1451 sizes the target from drawable.texture and reallocates on mismatch).
Actionable shape: one extra encoder + private composed target keyed by eye_id, entered from the 48/51
window, sampled by the unchanged MERC bind path. Still build-gated.

Addendum 8 (2026-09-14, host-only read-only) - Sky ALPHA 0x48 decode contradiction RESOLVED; no decoder change in scope.
Question: does GS ALPHA 0x48 decode to the additive branch (Cs-0)*As+Cd as the authority states, or is that decode wrong?
Evidence:
 (1) Port layout common/dma/gs.h:205-220: a=bits0-1, b=bits2-3, c=bits4-5, d=bits6-7; 0x48=0b01001000 ->
     a=0 SOURCE, b=2 ZERO_OR_FIXED, c=0 SOURCE, d=1 DEST.
 (2) GsAlpha::print() (gs.cpp:282-341) labels c_mode SOURCE as "As / 128.0" and d_mode DEST as " + Cd", so it
     DOES fix the a,b,c,d field order, and that order is the additive form.
 (3) DirectRenderer.cpp:1548 BlendState::from_register assigns a=reg.a_mode()..d=reg.d_mode() with NO repacking
     or bit shuffle, so the desktop oracle sees this exact layout; the matching branch is DirectRenderer.cpp:509-517
     (SOURCE,ZERO_OR_FIXED,SOURCE,DEST) = glBlendFuncSeparate(SRC_ALPHA, ONE, ONE, ZERO) + FUNC_ADD = Cs*As + Cd.
 (4) Raw hardware GS ALPHA bits agree: 0x48 bits4-5=00=As (not Ad), so hardware also yields (Cs-0)*As + Cd.
 (5) The prior "literal hardware decode 0x48 = c=1 Ad" reading is an arithmetic slip: bit4 of 0x48 is 0, not 1.
     No convention (port, desktop, hardware) produces Ad for 0x48, so the (Cs-0)*Ad+Cd hypothesis is refuted.
Control: 0x44 (ledger batches 0-3, the sky base) = a=SOURCE,b=DEST,c=SOURCE,d=DEST = source-over, which native
already does, so the refuted hypothesis would have changed nothing for those batches.
 (6) DrawMode (gs.h:417-430) mirrors the same table: AlphaBlend 1=SRC_DST_SRC_DST (0x44), 2=SRC_0_SRC_DST (0x48);
     native maps enum 2 to source-over - the TIE-side half of the same fix.
Ledger: /private/tmp/ottsel-recovery-2026-09-12/sky-population-product-capture/ottsel-sky-population-ledger.txt
 (4x ALPHA=44, 37x ALPHA=48; 35x blend=1, 6x blend=0; the 6 blend=0 are batches 35-40 where ALPHA is irrelevant).
Conclusion: the authority decode is correct and was only under-specified (field order not shown). The ALPHA carrier
NativeSpriteBatch.diagnostic_alpha (metal_shell.mm:334/2803) already holds the needed value, and the TIE path already
extracts the 3-bit AlphaBlend; the only product change is applying the additive blend for 0x48 in both decoders.

Addendum 9 (2026-09-14, host-only read-only) - Sky/TIE additive blend fix site pinned to pipeline selection, no decoder edit.
Question: what exactly must change to make ALPHA 0x48 (Direct) and DrawMode::AlphaBlend 2 (TIE) render additively?
Finding: Metal bakes blend factors into the pipeline state, and the shell already owns both carriers, so the change is two
new additive pipeline states + mode-based selection, with zero decoder or data-format work.
 TIE path:
  - selection site metal_shell.mm:1752 computes alpha_blend_enabled = ottsel_mobile_alpha_blend_enabled((draw.mode&(1<<20))!=0,
    (draw.mode>>24)&7u); the 3-bit AlphaBlend mode IS read here.
  - mobile_scene_policy.h:120-124 collapses it to a bool: returns abe && mode!=0 && mode!=6, so enum 2 (SRC_0_SRC_DST,
    additive) and enum 1 (SRC_DST_SRC_DST, source-over) are indistinguishable downstream.
  - metal_shell.mm:1039-1044 bakes source-over into a single tieBlendPipeline (SourceAlpha, OneMinusSourceAlpha, Add,
    One, Zero); :1764-1765 selects tieBlendPipeline vs tiePipeline. There is no additive TIE state.
  - fix: add a third TIE state (SourceAlpha, One, Add, One, Zero) and select it when abe && mode==2.
 Direct/Sky path:
  - metal_shell.mm:925-930 bakes source-over into directBlendPipeline; :2350-2351 selects directBlendPipeline vs
    directPipeline from direct_batch.blended.
  - the raw ALPHA register already travels as NativeSpriteBatch.diagnostic_alpha (metal_gfx.mm:3388 -> metal_shell.mm:2803,
    field :334), so decoding SOURCE/ZERO_OR_FIXED/SOURCE/DEST at selection time needs no new plumbing.
  - fix: same shape - an additive direct state selected when the register decodes to b==ZERO_OR_FIXED with a==c==SOURCE, d==DEST.
Correction to the prior scope note: the earlier 'the fix needs both decoders' phrasing is imprecise; both decoders already
emit the needed value (raw register for Direct, 3-bit mode for TIE), and the only production edits are in metal_shell.mm
pipeline creation/selection. Still build-gated; no product build ran.

Addendum 10 (2026-09-14, host-only read-only) - gate 2 first item already true in current source.
Authority gate 2 says: 'surgically remove the unconditional production direct_fs diagnostic return'.
Current-source check (no build): the production fragment function direct_fs (metal_shell.mm:79-110) has no diagnostic
early return - it samples the texel, substitutes GS TA0 for a zero texel alpha, applies the TFX/TCC modulation, applies
the alpha test and returns color. The constant/diagnostic returns exist only in separately named functions:
direct_fs_solid (magenta, metal_shell.mm:112), direct_fs_debug_fill (:115), direct_fs_debug_tex (:120).
Those are wired into distinct pipelines that are all gated behind diagnostic flags: directColorOnlyPipeline
(selected only when self.directFixtureActive && self.directFixtureColorOnly, :2377), skyDebugFillPipeline and
skyDebugTexPipeline (built from directBlendPd copies, :956-967, used by the sky-isolation harness), and the
directFixtureOffscreen render target (:1441-1458, default NO, reset at :3566). The production pipelines
(directPipeline/directBlendPipeline, :908/:925) bind the real direct_fs fragment.
Conclusion: gate 2's direct_fs item needs no source change; the only gate-2 work remaining is the parts that require a
build (compile from known source, record executable hash / data identity / device / route / flags / timestamps, and the
default-on probe audit), all still blocked by the disk gate. No product build ran.

